  class="fullscreen-section case-study-section"
  data-title="Case Study: Pet Art With Heart — Web &amp; Interaction Design | Katja Turnsek"
  data-description="UX/UI case study for a real-world pet portrait business, focused on artwork hierarchy, trust, commission pathways, and responsive gallery design."
  data-case="petart"
  aria-labelledby="case-petart-design-title"
>
  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-header">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Web &amp; Interaction Design</p>
        <h1 id="case-petart-design-title">Pet Art With Heart</h1>
        <p class="case-subtitle">An artwork-led experience that introduces a
          personal commission service with warmth, clarity, and trust.</p>

        <dl class="case-summary" aria-label="Project summary">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand, UX/UI, content, and art direction</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent real-world business site</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>Ongoing</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Figma, wireframing, responsive UI, visual identity</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Live commission portfolio</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper">
          <div
            class="demo-links"
            data-auto="demo"
            data-case="petart-design"
          ></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class="project-switcher"
          aria-label="Pet Art With Heart sections"
          data-auto="switcher"
          data-case="petart"
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Pet Art With Heart needed to work as both an artist portfolio and the
        first step in a commission relationship. The design had to let the
        portraits create an emotional first impression while helping visitors
        understand the service, feel confident in the artist, and know how to
        make contact.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The artwork needed visual space, but a gallery alone would not
            answer the practical and emotional questions behind a custom
            portrait. The experience had to balance storytelling with
            information and guide visitors toward a commission without feeling
            sales-heavy.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Keep the portraits as the strongest visual element</li>
            <li>Build trust through personal context, process information, and
              clear next steps</li>
            <li>Create a distinctive identity that does not compete with
              different artwork styles</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-petartmoodboard-600.webp"
            data-src="design-petartmoodboard-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="517"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Pet Art With Heart mood board showing colours, typography, and visual inspiration"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Mood board balancing warmth, craft,
            and a restrained gallery setting</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Used generous whitespace and quiet typography so detailed
              portraits remain the main focus across the site
            </li>
            <li>
              Structured the content around artwork, artist story, commission
              context, and contact rather than presenting disconnected gallery
              pages
            </li>
            <li>
              Repeated clear but understated calls to action at natural decision
              points instead of relying on a single contact link
            </li>
            <li>
              Kept interaction subtle so hover and motion cues support
              navigation without competing with the emotional character of the
              work
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-petartprototype-600.webp"
            data-src="design-petartprototype-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="439"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Pet Art With Heart desktop prototype showing an artwork-led page layout"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">High-fidelity prototype with
            artwork-led hierarchy and clear content sections</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block">
          <h2>From structure to presentation</h2>
          <p>
            Wireframes were used to establish reading order and content rhythm
            before the artwork and visual identity were applied. This made it
            easier to test whether the site still made sense without relying on
            decorative styling.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Planned a simple route from first impression to service
              understanding and contact</li>
            <li>Alternated gallery moments with concise explanatory content</li>
            <li>Adjusted image scale and content stacking for narrower screens</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-petartwireframes-900.webp"
            data-src="design-petartwireframes-900.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="900"
            height="574"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Pet Art With Heart desktop wireframes showing the planned content hierarchy"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Wireframes establishing content order
            before visual styling</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final experience gives the artwork room to create an emotional
        connection while the supporting content explains the real commission
        service clearly. Because this is my own ongoing art business, the design
        also has to remain practical to update as new portraits and information
        are added. It strengthened how I connect brand expression with a
        focused, trustworthy user journey.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="back-to-work-container">
      <a
        href="{{base}}#work"
        class="label-link back-to-work-label"
        data-back
        data-back-fallback="{{base}}#work"
        aria-label="Back to Work"
      >
        <svg class="icon icon-18 arrow-icon" aria-hidden="true"><use
            href="#i-arrow-left"
          /></svg>
        <span class="label-text">Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>